{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Mirror inventory finding F10 (`reports/stability/mirror-inventory.md`), MEDIUM, ALREADY DRIFTED.\n\n## Verified drift (all three re-checked against the tree, not taken from the inventory)\n\n1. `docs/reuse-surface.md` verdict table lists **8** verdicts; `verify_signature_result.schema.json`\n   declares **9** — `key_not_valid_at_era` is absent. It IS documented at `docs/identity.md:138,147`,\n   so the omission is staleness, not a deliberate exclusion.\n2. `docs/oss-comparison-and-remediation.md:110-113` \"Native model for reference\" gives types\n   `task|story|bug|epic` (master has 7 — `session_log`, `code_review`, `identity` missing), statuses\n   `open|in_progress|blocked|closed|archived|deleted` (master has 7 — `idea` missing), and the words\n   \"six relations\" where `Relation` declares **seven**.\n3. `docs/user-guide.md:147` says \"Statuses are `idea | open | in_progress | closed | blocked`\" — 5 of\n   7, omitting `archived` and `deleted`, which the SAME file then discusses at `:560,568-571`.\n\nMasters: `src/rebar/types.py:21` (`TicketStatus`), `:23` (`TicketType`), `:24` (`Relation`), and\n`src/rebar/schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json` (verdicts) — read through\n`rebar.schemas.load(\"verify_signature_result\")` rather than by literal path. None of the three\nsites is generated or gated: `gen_api_surface.py` gates library SYMBOL NAMES only and never reads\ndoc prose.\n\n## The arms, counted exactly\n\nFIVE enumerations across THREE documents, and they do not share a parse shape — which is why the\ncensus registers arms, not files:\n\n| # | Document | Enumeration | Shape in the prose | Master |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `reuse-surface.md` | verdicts | markdown table, backticked first column | schema enum |\n| 2 | `oss-comparison-and-remediation.md` | types | one span, `a\\|b\\|c` no spaces | `TicketType` |\n| 3 | `oss-comparison-and-remediation.md` | statuses | one span, `a\\|b\\|c` no spaces | `TicketStatus` |\n| 4 | `oss-comparison-and-remediation.md` | relation COUNT | the English word \"six\" | `len(Relation)` |\n| 5 | `user-guide.md` | statuses | one span, `a \\| b \\| c` with spaces | `TicketStatus` |\n\nArm 4 is the odd one: it is a number word in prose, not a list, so it is checked as a COUNT against\n`len(get_args(Relation))` and corrected to \"seven\". Registering it as a list arm would be wrong.\n\n## One honest complication, handled rather than ignored\n\nThe `reuse-surface.md` table is introduced by \"Common verdicts include:\" — a hedge that makes the\ntable non-exhaustive BY CONSTRUCTION. A completeness gate over prose that only claims to be partial\nwould assert something the text never promised. So the fix corrects the prose to a complete\nenumeration and the gate then pins that promise. The same applies to the `user-guide.md` line, which\nstates a flat \"Statuses are …\". Where a doc genuinely wants a partial list it keeps the hedge and is\nsimply not registered in the census — being in the census is what makes it a promise.\n\n## Approach — add-parity-check\n\nCorrect all three texts, then add `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_docs_enumeration_parity.py`\npinning each corrected enumeration to its code master. The test holds a registry of LOCATIONS —\n(doc path, an anchor that finds the enumeration, the master to compare against) — and never a copy\nof the VALUES, which are always read from `types.py` / the schema at assert time. That is the same\nshape as the existing `test_event_schema_relations.py` docs arm, extended to three more sites; a\nregistry of values would just relocate the mirror this ticket exists to remove.\n\nHouse style is per-topic contract tests (`test_event_schema_relations.py`,\n`test_creation_channel_vocabulary.py`, `test_bridge_vocabulary_stale_heldout.py`); there is no\ngeneric docs-enumeration census to extend, so this adds the first one.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `docs/reuse-surface.md` lists all 9 verdicts and no longer hedges with \"Common verdicts\n      include\". Verified by the census comparing the table against the schema enum and by reading\n      the rendered table.\n- [ ] AC2: `docs/oss-comparison-and-remediation.md` names all 7 types, all 7 statuses, and \"seven\n      relations\". Verified by the census against `TicketType`, `TicketStatus` and `Relation`.\n- [ ] AC3: `docs/user-guide.md:147` names all 7 statuses. Verified by the census against\n      `TicketStatus`.\n- [ ] AC4: the census FAILS when any one registered arm drifts, and its message names the document,\n      the arm, and the missing or extra values (for arm 4, the expected and actual count). Verified\n      by FIVE mutation checks, one per arm in the table above, each RED with the offending value or\n      count in the message.\n- [ ] AC5: the census reads every expected value from the code master at assert time; no relation,\n      status, type or verdict is spelled out in the test. Verified by review of the test source and\n      by adding a value to a master and seeing the DOC arm fail (never a copy inside the test —\n      there is none). A parse that stops matching must report \"could not locate\", not an empty set,\n      so a reworded passage blames the parser rather than the values.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/contracts/test_docs_enumeration_parity.py -q` — AC1-AC5.\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/contracts -q` — no regression in the sibling contract tests.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788403852958133001,"uuid":"8a122d03-6bff-4471-82c6-a2a1d1f43c52"}